Reported by Tom: refreshing a big project updates every issue's Remedy field text (good) -- but Sending that project to a copy of it in a different, not-yet-refreshed instance then says there are no changes to merge, even though Remedy clearly differs.

Root cause found in mergeIssuePair (wigwag.html, ~line 2495). Its touchedFields computation reuses localAuthored/incomingAuthored, both already filtered to origin !== 'derived':

  const localAuthored = localIssue.history.filter(h => h.field === colId && h.origin !== 'derived');
  const incomingAuthored = incomingIssue.history.filter(h => h.field === colId && h.origin !== 'derived');
  ...
  if (incomingOnly) touchedFields.push(colId);

That exclusion is correct, and deliberate, for overlappingFields -- the comment right above the function explains it exists so two independently-recomputed bound values disagreeing isn't flagged as an authorship conflict. But touchedFields reuses the exact same pre-filtered sets for an entirely different purpose: deciding whether the incoming side brought ANY real change at all. touchedFields feeds mergeIssueSummaries, which is what mergeHasRealChanges (the gate behind "no changes to merge") checks first.

Remedy is presumably a rule-bound field (def.linkedSourceId set) -- its value only ever gets written via logDerivedChanges/computeDerivedChangeEntries, always tagged origin:'derived' (confirmed: that's the one and only place origin:'derived' gets stamped). So a refresh that changes Remedy purely as a derived side-effect of some other field's real update writes history entries that are invisible to touchedFields -- the field never gets into mergeIssueSummaries, and if nothing else on the issue changed in a non-derived way, mergeHasRealChanges returns false for the whole project: "no changes to merge", despite every Remedy value in the file actually differing.

Fix (not yet applied -- wigwag.html is mid-edit for an unrelated Tauri notification fix and a full regression suite is currently running against that; will apply this right after, as its own separate, separately-verified change): compute touchedFields from each field's FULL history (both origins), independently of the already-correct derived-excluding sets used for overlappingFields -- i.e. two separate local/incoming key-sets per field, one unfiltered (for touchedFields) and one derived-excluding (for overlappingFields), rather than one set serving both. overlappingFields' own behavior is untouched by this.
